<p><br></p>
<h1 id=3D"AccessControlintheUI-Implementation">Implementation</h1>
<p><br></p>
<p><strong>Singleton Service</strong><br>
  All access control across the the UI managed by Compass-X, goes through a=
 Singleton Service which is shared across micro-front-ends. This created a =
single source of truth for the logic for managing access, the AccessControl=
Service.<br><br><strong>Access Control UI Cache</strong><br>
  Since there is no limit to the amount of "resources" or "permissions" whi=
ch need to be checked within the UI, and there is a lot of repetition of th=
e same resource or scope being checked in multiple places. We needed to imp=
lement a short lived in-memory cache to prevent calling the same endpoint m=
ultiple times within the same minute, otherwise the UI would load at the pa=
ce of Windows 98.&nbsp;<br>
  We created a short-lived cache within the AccessControlService for all AP=
I calls, where the cache key is a combination of either the "resource" or "=
permission" and the access token of the user.&nbsp;<br><br>
  The time a "resource" or "permission" access of a user remains in cache i=
s currently set to 5 mins</p>
<p><br>
  The cache is purged in 3 ways:</p>
<ul style=3D"list-style-type: square;">
 <li>Whenever there is a change in token</li>
 <li>The user logs in/out</li>
 <li>Refresh the page / Close the tab</li>
</ul>
<p>There is also a sweep of the cache to delete expired checks at every 5 m=
ins to avoid memory leaks.<br><br><br></p>
<p><strong>Bundled Calls</strong></p>
<p>All access control checks trigger async parallel calls to the <span styl=
e=3D"color:#172b4d;">G12&nbsp;</span>and R9 endpoints if the individual api=
 calls are not already in cache. The access-control check only completes wh=
en we receive the final API response.</p>
<p><br></p>
<h1 id=3D"AccessControlintheUI-SequenceDiagrams">Sequence Diagrams</h1>
<p>For how the OKTA PKCE flow works please refer to the OKTA documentation:=
 <a class=3D"external-link" href=3D"https://developer.okta.com/docs/guides/=
implement-grant-type/authcodepkce/main/" rel=3D"nofollow">https://developer=
.okta.com/docs/guides/implement-grant-type/authcodepkce/main/</a><br>
  Here's an additional sequence diagram on the PKCE flow in case it's helps=
<br><br><br></p>
<p><span class=3D"confluence-embedded-file-wrapper confluence-embedded-manu=
al-size"><img class=3D"confluence-embedded-image" draggable=3D"false" heigh=
t=3D"243" src=3D"2c58e48c76a78f1dd6dc105754c5c3fd2443f2947891676f5cfd17d9bc=
b1f19c" data-image-src=3D"/download/attachments/2148468745/pkce-flow-fca8d8=
16046e3b1ab1ebe4c95ca4c639f3e8a8aa36b94d07919e7dfd4c60dedf.png?version=3D1&=
amp;modificationDate=3D1738793182436&amp;api=3Dv2" data-unresolved-comment-=
count=3D"0" data-linked-resource-id=3D"2148470553" data-linked-resource-ver=
sion=3D"1" data-linked-resource-type=3D"attachment" data-linked-resource-de=
fault-alias=3D"pkce-flow-fca8d816046e3b1ab1ebe4c95ca4c639f3e8a8aa36b94d0791=
9e7dfd4c60dedf.png" data-base-url=3D"https://wiki.bnc.ca" data-linked-resou=
rce-content-type=3D"image/png" data-linked-resource-container-id=3D"2148468=
745" data-linked-resource-container-version=3D"6" tabindex=3D"0" alt=3D"" w=
idth=3D"468"></span></p>
<p><br></p>
<p>When the Client app has an access-token stored in it's session, we're th=
en able to do the access control calls in the UI.<br><br><span class=3D"con=
fluence-embedded-file-wrapper confluence-embedded-manual-size"><img class=
=3D"confluence-embedded-image confluence-thumbnail" draggable=3D"false" hei=
ght=3D"476" src=3D"91dc4403d8237ef6cafebf91d5b7c4f7d5216b1cc7a23c65e59da129=
4727c4b1" data-image-src=3D"/download/attachments/2148468745/Screenshot%202=
025-02-05%20at%205.14.48%E2%80%AFPM.png?version=3D1&amp;modificationDate=3D=
1738793699599&amp;api=3Dv2" data-unresolved-comment-count=3D"0" data-linked=
-resource-id=3D"2148470584" data-linked-resource-version=3D"1" data-linked-=
resource-type=3D"attachment" data-linked-resource-default-alias=3D"Screensh=
